! lve_indirect.strap -- LVE, Load Value Effective: indirect addressing.
!
! WHY THIS EXISTS.  MCP's bootstrap pins LVE's ENCODING -- printed p.3
! line 10 assembles bit-for-bit -- and nothing pinned its EXECUTION.
! The two are separately earned, which is the whole point of
! audit_opcode_coverage.py's ENCODING_ONLY category; this is the
! program that moves LVE out of it.
!
! WHAT LVE DOES.  Reference Manual printed p.29, read first-hand:
! the effective address names a HALF WORD, that half word is decoded
! far enough to work out ITS effective address, and THAT is what goes
! into the index register named by the original instruction's J field.
! The target's own operation is not executed and its own J is ignored.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SHAPE OF THE TEST, and why all three answers are the same
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The address field of the target is 24, 19 or 18 bits wide depending
! on the target's instruction class, and the manual (printed p.21)
! says the short ones are "extended with the necessary low-order
! zeros".  So for a target that addresses word W:
!
!   full word (24 bits)   field = W*64   effective = W*64
!   half word (19 bits)   field = W*2    effective = field << 5 = W*64
!   floating point (18)   field = W      effective = field << 6 = W*64
!
! ALL THREE COME OUT THE SAME NUMBER, which is exactly what makes this
! a test of the width logic rather than of one case: point three
! targets of three different classes at ONE datum and every LVE must
! produce that datum's bit address.  Get a width wrong and the answer
! is off by a factor of 32 or 64, not by a little.
!
! The widths themselves are not invented here.  They are the rule
! StretchDecode's svaReplaceAddr already applies, from jb_ax3a printed
! p.140's own six-case table, and this program is the first thing to
! exercise it in the LVE direction rather than the SVA one.

        SLC, (8)2400.0

! ---- a full-word target: 24-bit address ----------------------------
START   LVE, $X1, T24
        SV,  $X1, R24

! ---- a half-word branch target: 19-bit address ---------------------
        LVE, $X1, T19
        SV,  $X1, R19

! ---- a floating-point target: 18-bit address -----------------------
        LVE, $X1, T18
        SV,  $X1, R18

! ---- the target's OWN index field is honoured ----------------------
! p.29: the final effective address is produced "according to the
! rules which apply to that instruction", and address modification is
! one of those rules.  TIDX names $X3, so the answer must be the
! datum's address PLUS $X3's value.
        LX,  $X3, XOFF
        LVE, $X1, TIDX
        SV,  $X1, RIDX

! ---- the target's OWN J field is IGNORED ---------------------------
! TJ is a direct-index instruction naming $X7.  p.29: "The fields J of
! all subsequent instructions encountered in the operation are
! ignored."  So $X7 must be untouched and the answer must land in $X1.
        LVE, $X1, TJ
        SV,  $X1, RJ
        SV,  $X7, RJ7

! ---- a CHAIN is refused out loud -----------------------------------
! p.29 makes a chained LVE an iteration with a one-millisecond bound
! and indicator USA.  This E-box runs one instruction per step with no
! inner loop, so StretchExecute refuses rather than silently
! truncating to one level -- see its own comment.  OP must come on.
        LVE, $X1, TLVE
        BZB, $OP, $                ! hang up if OP is off
        B,   PASS

! ---- the targets ---------------------------------------------------
! All four of the first group address DATUM and differ only in class.
T24     L(BU,8), DATUM             ! VFL: full word, 24-bit address
T19     B,   DATUM                 ! unconditional branch: 19 bits
T18     L(N), DATUM                ! floating point: 18 bits
TIDX    L(BU,8), DATUM($X3)        ! full word, modified by $X3
TJ      LV,  $X7, DATUM            ! direct index: 19 bits, J = 7
TLVE    LVE, $X7, DATUM            ! itself an LVE: a chain

XOFF    XW,  (8)1000.0, 0, 0, 0    ! the index value TIDX adds
DATUM   DD(BU,64,8), 0
R24     VF,  0
R19     VF,  0
R18     VF,  0
RIDX    VF,  0
RJ      VF,  0
RJ7     VF,  0
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
